{"records":[{"id":"3ctpx4k6jp","title":"Porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome virus upregulates SMPDL3B to promote viral replication by modulating lipid metabolism","description":"We generated a collection of porcine genome-scale CRISPR/Cas9 knockout (PigGeCKO) cells on a PAM background.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-26T18:53:54.241Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3ctpx4k6jp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3ctpx4k6jp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"huanh","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Agricultural Biotechnology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pm5v6pwkkg","title":"Genome-wide screens implicate loss of cullin ring ligase 3 in persistent proliferation and genome instability in TP53 deficient cells","description":"TP53 deficiency is the most common alteration in cancer, however, alone is typically insufficient to drive tumorigenesis. To identify genes promoting tumorigenesis in combination with TP53 deficiency, we performed genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens coupled with proliferation and transformation assays in isogenic cell lines. Loss of several known tumor suppressor genes enhanced cellular proliferation and transformation. Surprisingly, loss neddylation pathway genes, promoted uncontrolled proliferation exclusively in TP53-deficient cells. CUL3 coupled with TP53 loss results in an oncogenic transcriptional program governed by the NFκB, AP-1 and TGF-β pathways. This program maintains persistent cellular proliferation, induces partial epithelial to mesenchymal transition, increases DNA damage, genomic instability, and the rate chromosomal rearrangements. Our findings reveal CUL3 loss as a key event stimulating persistent proliferation in TP53 deficient cells. These findings may be clinically relevant, since TP53-CUL3 deficient cells are highly sensitive to ATM inhibition, exposing a vulnerability that could be exploited for cancer treatment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T17:31:10.704Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pm5v6pwkkg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pm5v6pwkkg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexandros","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4g722x4jfg","title":"Western blot","description":"Western blot of CRISPR-Cas9-mediated nontargeting (NT) versus BACH2 knockout 3 days after CRISPR-Cas9-gRNA nucleofection of human gut cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-02T21:20:55.966Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4g722x4jfg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4g722x4jfg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yulong","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gut","Western Blot","T Cell","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xv35nccf5g","title":"usp8 Meiding","description":"The CRISPR/Cas9 genome-editing tool was used to knock out the expression of USP8 (USP8-KO) in SUM159 cells. Knockout of USP8 was confirmed by western blot analysis using antibodies against USP8 and GAPDH. Raw data and raw data with molecular weigh labelled.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-30T20:10:51.846Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xv35nccf5g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xv35nccf5g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mei","last_name":"Ding","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3xts2ms935","title":"Exosomal miR-145-5p promotes apoptosis of renal tubule epithelial cells through the JNK signalling pathway. Pu Mao et al","description":"This dataset contains the files (data and codes) needed to replicate our results in the study titled as \" Exosomal miR-145-5p promotes apoptosis of renal tubule epithelial cells through the JNK signalling pathway \".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-08T09:58:24.047Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3xts2ms935","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3xts2ms935"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pu","last_name":"Mao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"81770077"}],"categories":["RNA Gene","Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"btjbcz6hpr","title":"Yahia Mjery - Identification of novel IgA receptors expressed by human mesangial cells and proximal tubule epithelial cells","description":"The attached supplemental table here show the large number of identified proteins in IgA-containing immune complexes isolated from the lysates of mesangial cells and proximal tubule epithelial cells after being incubated with or without IgA from healthy people and IgA nephropathy patients ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-23T08:01:46.909Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/btjbcz6hpr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/btjbcz6hpr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yahia","last_name":"Mjery","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Chronic Kidney Disease"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vzr4snpdb4","title":"SR8278 inhibits cell proliferation independent of REV-ERB","description":"SR8278 has been reported to be an antagonist of the REV-ERB proteins. In this work, we treated HaCaT cells with SR8278 and used RNA-seq to identify genes that were significantly altered by treatment. Genes involved in cell proliferation and the G1/S phase transition were a major group of altered genes. We confirmed the RNA-seq results with RT-qPCR and western blotting. Furthermore, SR8278 treatment slowed the growth of HaCaT and other cell lines. Using CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing, we generated REV-ERB alpha, REV-ERB beta, and REV-ERB alpha/beta double-knockout HaCaT cells and found that SR8278's effects were independent of REV-ERB. The data include those generated by RT-qPCR, western blotting, and cell viability assays.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-11T15:43:52.8Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vzr4snpdb4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vzr4snpdb4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Kemp","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05dbx6743","name":"Department of Veterans Affairs","grant_id":"I01CX002241"},{"id":"https://ror.org/04q48ey07","name":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","grant_id":"R01GM130583"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00wzt9y79","name":"Ohio Cancer Research Associates","grant_id":"5020"}],"categories":["Pharmacology","Nuclear Receptor","Transcription Factor","Small Molecule","Keratinocyte"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":107103,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":142,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":107103,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":106642,"restricted":53,"embargoed":408},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1878},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":568},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":423},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":364},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":362},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":359},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":333},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":297},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":231},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":193}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":104589},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1885},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":168},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":110},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":65},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":62},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":45},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":29},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":27},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":443},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":379},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9